zdani" : order and cmavo (particles) make the difference.

> How do you form adjectives?

There are no "adjectives" per se. You qualify sumti using relative=20
clauses, or change the meaning of relationships using tanru grouping.

> How do you include the objects of nouns?

What does this mean ?

> How can you tell when a compound phrase arnt just two words in=20
> different positions?

There are cmavo to do that.

For instance : "mi cmalu prenu" - "cmalu prenu" is the brivla (relation=20=

phrase) because nothing comes in between to break it apart. Compare=20
with "mi klama le zdani".

> How do you form adverbs?

There are no "adverbs" per se. Most relation words have sumti "slots"=20
to add adverb-like meaning. If some slot is missing for the idea you=20
want to involve in a relationship, you can add it using a so-called BAI=20=

clause.

> What are the logical and set operators, and how do they work=20
> semantically?

The logical operators are those that help you say "this AND this" or=20
"that OR those", as well as express negation and affirmation, and this=20=

kind of things.

The set operators are those that allow you to express what groups of=20
things are composed of.

> When talking about something in the plural, is it by default=20
> considered to function as a set or as each thing?

There is no plural unless you explicit a number greater than 1. Then,=20
it depends on how you make the plural thing into a sumti : you can do=20
both sets or masses, depending on the cmavo you use.

> How do you specify the degree of a word?

What do you mean ?

> How do you talk about things like ownership and belongingship?

These are expressed using relative clauses (the home that belongs to=20
me).

> How do you talk about physical relation and motion?

Relationship is expressed using phrases (brivla), and motion like tense=20=

is expressed using cmavo that modify the meaning of the relation-words.

